## Outcome

**Used, and people keep coming back**

After the launch peak of about 1,600 users, a steady base settled in: around 800 a month through 2024, and 450 to 630 through 2026. About 8 in 10 of them are returning users, and alerts have been opened from notifications over 100,000 times in three years.

{% usagechart caption="Monthly active and returning users since launch, rounded. Test and visitor traffic left out." /%}

{% block source="23 of 29 rated ease of use 4 or 5 (average 4.1/5) · post-launch survey of 31 partners" %}
Most partners found the app easy to use.
{% /block %}

**What I can't say.** Nothing could be measured before the app, since SMS and email can't be tracked, so I can't say {% mark text="how much impact it had on the safety and security of humanitarian workers" /%}, only that it's used and that people keep coming back. The partner survey below is the asking side, the support layer.

{% cta href="https://app.ngosafety.org/" label="Take a look" /%}

## Takeaway

**Ask for the users early, and plan around what you get**

I couldn't reach partners directly, so country office colleagues stood in, generously and not always easily. Next time I'd make the case for end-user access from the first week, with a clear idea of what it would change. Where it still isn't possible, I'd plan the research around that limit, and accept that some decisions come without their reasons.

{% cta href="/work/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/full/" label="Read the full case study (13 min)" /%}
